# Python testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing guidance to `pytest`
conventions. Applies only to `*.py`/`*.pyi` files; see
`skills/python-testing/SKILL.md` for the full test-authoring workflow this
rule file backs.

## Layout and discovery

- Match the project's existing layout: a `tests/` tree mirroring `src/`, or
  co-located `test_*.py`/`*_test.py` files — do not introduce the other
  layout alongside it.
- Name test files, classes, and functions so `pytest`'s default discovery
  finds them (`test_*.py`/`*_test.py`, `Test*` classes with no `__init__`,
  `test_*` functions) rather than relying on manual `testpaths` overrides.
- Keep a fixture in the narrowest `conftest.py` that covers every test
  needing it; do not hoist a single-file fixture to the suite root.

## Fixtures and parametrization

- Prefer the pytest default `function` scope; widen to `module`/`session`
  only for expensive, read-only setup, and never for a fixture that mutates
  shared state.
- Use `@pytest.mark.parametrize` when only inputs vary; use a fixture (or
  `pytest.fixture(params=[...])`) when setup/teardown logic itself varies
  between cases.
- Give parametrized cases explicit `ids=` when the parameter values are not
  self-describing in pytest's default test-ID output.

## Mocking

- Patch at the point of use (`mocker.patch("mypkg.module.dependency")`), not
  at the dependency's definition site.
- Mock only external dependencies (network, filesystem, other services,
  wall-clock time); an internal collaborator mocked away stops the test
  verifying real integration between your own modules.
- Prefer `unittest.mock.AsyncMock` (or `pytest-mock`'s `mocker.patch` with
  `new=AsyncMock()`) for mocking an `async def` dependency, not a plain
  `Mock` that returns a coroutine-shaped object nothing ever awaits.

## Async tests

- Mark an async test with `pytest.mark.asyncio` (or the project's configured
  async plugin/mode) — a sync test function that merely calls a coroutine
  without awaiting it silently never runs the coroutine's body.
- Use the project's configured event-loop fixture scope; do not create a new
  event loop per test unless the project's async plugin requires it.

## Determinism

- Never depend on real wall-clock time, network access, or filesystem state
  outside a fixture-managed temp directory (`tmp_path`) — freeze time
  (`freezegun` or the project's equivalent) and mock the network.
- Never depend on dict/set iteration order for test correctness beyond
  Python's own guaranteed dict insertion order; sort collections before
  comparing when the underlying data structure does not guarantee order.
- A flaky test (passes/fails nondeterministically) gets fixed at its root
  cause (a missing await, an unmocked clock, a race) — never retried into
  passing or skipped to hide the flake.

## Coverage expectations

- Every new or touched public function/class/endpoint gets at least one
  test covering its happy path, its documented edge cases, and any raised
  exception type.
- A test asserts one behavior; a test with several unrelated assertions
  should split so a failure names the specific behavior that broke.
- Run the project's configured coverage tool (`pytest --cov` when
  configured) and treat an uncovered branch in touched code as a gap to
  close, not to suppress with a `# pragma: no cover` on real logic.
